<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The roots the FileProvider (declared in AndroidManifest.xml, authority
  `${applicationId}.files`) may mint content:// URIs inside. Its only consumers
  are the "open with" handoffs on the series detail screen — grep `openBook` in
  ui/screens/SeriesDetailScreen.kt.

  DECLARATION ORDER IS NOT PRECEDENCE. FileProvider keeps its roots in a
  HashMap and picks the LONGEST matching prefix
  (SimplePathStrategy.getUriForFile), so the app-scoped entry always wins for a
  file under it even though the volumes entry below also contains it. The order
  here is for a reader, not for the resolver.

  1. external-files-path `manga/` — Context.getExternalFilesDir(null)/manga,
     which is Aio.defaultLibraryDir (grep `defaultLibraryDir` in core/Aio.kt).
     The no-permission default, and still the tightest declaration.

  2. root-path `/storage/` — every mounted volume: primary shared storage
     (/storage/emulated/0/…) and removable ones (/storage/XXXX-XXXX/…).
     M7 made the library root USER-CHOSEN (AppSettings.libraryPath, resolved by
     Aio.resolveLibraryDir), and a library moved to shared storage — the whole
     point of the MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE opt-in, since Komikku and Mihon
     cannot see inside an app-scoped directory — sits outside every scoped
     `*-path` element. Without this entry getUriForFile throws
     IllegalArgumentException and EVERY "open with" fails, for exactly the
     audience that storage flow courts.

     `/storage/` rather than <external-path> (which is only
     Environment.getExternalStorageDirectory(), i.e. the primary volume)
     because the Settings path field is free text and the writability verdict
     comes from aio_android.probe_library_root actually writing a file — so an
     SD-card root is reachable and would otherwise reproduce the same bug.
     It is NOT path="/": /data, /system and the app's own internal filesDir
     stay unnameable.

  WHAT THIS WIDENS, STATED HONESTLY. The previous scoping argument was that
  everything else under getExternalFilesDir is ours alone — the browser
  profiles holding a solved Cloudflare clearance, the vrf token cache,
  run_params.json — and that a provider path is a standing capability rather
  than a per-file one. Root 2 contains those too (they live under
  /storage/emulated/0/Android/data/<pkg>/files/…), and FileProvider has no
  exclusion mechanism, so ANY declaration broad enough to reach a user-chosen
  library root reaches them as well. That is acceptable because the capability
  is not reachable from outside the app: the provider is exported="false" with
  grantUriPermissions="true", so a URI exists only once OUR code mints one, for
  a path the library scan produced, handed out with a one-shot
  FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION on a single Intent. This widens what we CAN
  name; it does not widen what anyone else can ask for.
-->
<paths>
    <external-files-path name="library" path="manga/" />
    <root-path name="volumes" path="/storage/" />
</paths>
